export const debate = {"id":"albrecht-oppy-resurrection-ancient-christianity-2023","number":"78","assessmentModel":"5.6 Sol","assessmentRubric":"Slugfester Reassessment Rubric v2","title":"William Albrecht vs Graham Oppy: Resurrection and Ancient Christianity","label":"Resurrection and ancient Christianity","date":"2026-08-23","duration":"1 hr 03 min","youtubeUrl":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YzvidaGO3m4","motion":"Do Paul's letters, Gospel transmission, hostile witnesses, and early church structures make the bodily resurrection and Catholic continuity historically credible?","sourceNote":"Based on the complete debate transcript. Summaries are condensed for clarity, and quotations are kept brief and faithful to the source.","scoringNote":"Scores are AI-generated estimates of argumentative performance and are intended as transparent, revisable judgments rather than final verdicts.","summary":"Albrecht builds cumulative resurrection and ecclesial-continuity arguments, while Oppy presses visionary alternatives, transmission uncertainty, selective preservation, and early Christian plurality.","quotes":{"pro":{"text":"he uh believed in the bodily resurrection of Christ","context":"Albrecht characterizes the earliest undisputed Pauline letters as evidence for Paul's bodily-resurrection belief despite their limited narrative detail."},"con":{"text":"that's not his personal observations that's secondhand stuff","context":"Oppy distinguishes Paul's personal experience from inherited reports about the five hundred and other alleged witnesses."}},"sides":{"pro":{"name":"Catholic resurrection case","speaker":"William Albrecht","color":"teal"},"con":{"name":"Historical skepticism","speaker":"Graham Oppy","color":"coral"}},"score":{"pro":76,"con":83},"sections":[{"sectionId":"section-pauline-resurrection-and-appearances","title":"Pauline Evidence for Bodily Resurrection and Appearances","timebox":"4:25–12:02","score":{"pro":82,"con":88},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-paul-early-bodily-resurrection","time":"4:25","role":"Load-bearing constructive","words":"Paul's earliest undisputed letters, despite limited narrative detail, indicate that he believed Christ was bodily resurrected and appeared to him.","score":83,"critique":"Strongest feature: The chronologically early Pauline foundation directly advances the indispensable bodily-resurrection route, carefully distinguishes undisputed letters from contested authorship, acknowledges limited descriptive detail, and proportionately claims evidence for Paul's belief rather than pretending to supply an exhaustive resurrection narrative. Principal limitation: The excerpt identifies neither a particular passage nor the linguistic reasoning required to move from resurrection vocabulary and appearance testimony to a specifically tangible encounter, while the opposing visionary interpretation remains substantially unevaluated. Live burden: The case still must demonstrate that Paul's terminology and reported experience discriminate bodily manifestation from sincere visionary experience instead of merely establishing an early resurrection conviction. Locked score: The strong band reflects excellent central relevance, recoverable qualifications, and coherent chronology alongside thinner textual substantiation and limited engagement with the strongest alternative interpretation.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-pauline-vision-underdetermination","time":"5:17","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Paul's early letters remain compatible with a vision and do not clearly describe a bodily meeting with someone restored from death.","score":88,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply challenges the affirmative's decisive inference, separating early resurrection belief from evidence that Paul's experience involved a tangible encounter between bodies, while presenting visionary compatibility as underdetermination rather than disproof. Principal limitation: Its textual assessment remains compressed because it examines no particular Pauline passage, leaves appearance language unexplained, and only briefly addresses the transformed-embodiment reading of First Corinthians 15. Live burden: The skeptical case must demonstrate that a visionary alternative fits the relevant wording and context comparably well, without neglecting evidence that spiritual embodiment could remain bodily. Locked score: The very strong band reflects exceptionally direct responsiveness, stable terminology, charitable targeting, and disciplined scope, tempered by a materially underdeveloped passage-by-passage evidential foundation.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-paul-transformed-tangible-body","time":"7:43","role":"Major direct reply","words":"First Corinthians 15 contrasts mortal and spiritual bodies yet, on Albrecht's reading, describes a transformed tangible body rather than immaterial existence.","score":81,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply addresses the visionary challenge through First Corinthians 15's contrast between mortal and spiritual bodies, acknowledges the immaterial interpretation, and presents transformed embodiment as an account preserving bodily continuity. Principal limitation: Its claim that no other reading is available outruns the excerpt's support, because spiritual, physical, and tangible remain insufficiently differentiated, while the alternative receives no linguistic or contextual analysis. Live burden: The affirmative must establish why Paul's terminology denotes transformed material embodiment and why that interpretation bears on the physical character of reported appearances, rather than resurrection doctrine alone. Locked score: The strong/competent band reflects decisive relevance and responsiveness, tempered by overstated exclusivity, terminological instability, and evidential exposition inadequate for the interpretive confidence asserted.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-pauline-secondhand-witness-reports","time":"11:00","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Paul's witness list is secondhand and could have supplied the interpretive framework through which he understood his own experience.","score":88,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply precisely distinguishes Paul's personal experience from inherited historical testimony about other witnesses, explaining that secondhand reports could structure his interpretation without alleging fabrication or certain psychological influence. Principal limitation: The filtering hypothesis remains evidentially underdeveloped because nothing establishes Paul's informational sequence, documented interactions with earlier witnesses, or reasons for accepting instead of independently confirming the inherited framework. Live burden: The skeptical account must specify how much corroborative independence disappears when transmitted testimony may preserve genuine early reports and Paul's experience remains separately substantively relevant. Locked score: The very strong band recognizes exceptional calibration, precision, responsiveness, and charitable restraint while retaining a consequential reservation about the still conjectural interpretive mechanism.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"section-gospel-and-textual-transmission","title":"Gospel Sources and Textual Transmission","timebox":"17:00–25:49","score":{"pro":65,"con":79},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-gospel-patristic-source-convergence","time":"23:18","role":"Supporting reply","words":"Patristic, Pauline, Gospel, and hostile-witness materials are presented as converging components of an early cumulative historical case.","score":65,"critique":"Strongest feature: The move advances a cumulative method, connecting Pauline material, Gospel traditions, patristic testimony, and external markers so the affirmative case need not rest on one evidential strand. Principal limitation: The excerpt mainly questions whether competing groups derive from Jesus and gestures toward hostile markers, leaving patristic sourcing partially recoverable while not answering unknown-source, authorial-selection, copying, or independence objections. Live burden: The affirmative must identify the testimony, establish dating and informational independence, and show that convergence does not result from shared transmission or retrospective selection. Locked score: The mixed band reflects central relevance and useful cumulative architecture, but also incomplete target contact, sparse source specification, unstable scope, missing qualification, and argumentative force beyond what the evidence establishes.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-unavailable-source-copying-uncertainty","time":"17:00","role":"Supporting constructive","words":"Known interpolations expose uncertainty where earlier versions and underlying sources are unavailable, preventing direct assessment of copying reliability.","score":76,"critique":"Strongest feature: The argument sharply distinguishes detectable interpolation, where earlier and later witnesses enable comparison, from unavailable-exemplar cases that resist direct evaluation, responsibly presenting missing comparators as uncertainty rather than affirmative evidence of corruption. Principal limitation: Its application remains insufficiently delimited because it identifies no disputed wording, transmission pathway, or corruption indicator affecting First Corinthians 15, leaving the point generally relevant to preservation but nonresponsive to the decisive Pauline exegetical premise. Live burden: The skeptic must show why this generalized possibility lowers confidence in the particular Pauline claim, rather than restating a universal constraint on ancient reconstruction. Locked score: The strong/competent band reflects coherent reasoning and calibration, counterbalanced by indirect target contact, incomplete specification, and overbroad application.","tags":[]}},{"pro":null,"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-gospel-compositional-delay-and-sources","time":"18:53","role":"Load-bearing constructive","words":"The interval before Gospel composition leaves the authors' sources, source contents, selection methods, and transmission reliability historically uncertain.","score":81,"critique":"Strongest feature: The argument distinguishes several concrete transmission uncertainties—compositional delay, unidentified sources, unknown contents, and authorial selection—while appropriately presenting them as limits on confidence rather than proof of unreliability. Principal limitation: It provides no comparative historical baseline or specific Gospel example showing unacceptable degradation across two generations, and it addresses Gospel transmission instead of the selected Pauline bodily-reading claim. Live burden: The skeptic must demonstrate how these uncertainties materially compromise particular resurrection traditions and outweigh early formulas, convergent sources, or ordinary ancient historiographical practice. Locked score: The strong band reflects central relevance, unusually precise and historically plausible uncertainty claims, explicit qualification, and charitable framing, tempered by indirect responsiveness and evidence that remains generalized rather than source-specific.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"section-hostile-witnesses-and-preservation","title":"Hostile Witnesses and Documentary Preservation","timebox":"38:10–41:39","score":{"pro":73,"con":83},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-limited-spread-does-not-defeat-identification","time":"39:38","role":"Major direct reply","words":"A hostile source can identify recognizable Christian beliefs without proving those beliefs were geographically universal or numerically dominant.","score":73,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply narrows the claim by arguing that early bishops, priests, and deacons, persisting amid conflict through Nicaea, can identify a structured community without requiring Christianity to have been prevalent. Principal limitation: Institutional persistence neither answers selective preservation nor establishes independence from Christian informants, and it cannot uniquely identify Catholic continuity rather than one community among several competing Christian lineages. Live burden: The affirmative must define the Catholic markers, show how outside testimony was acquired, and explain why continuity of offices supports exclusive apostolic and resurrection claims. Locked score: The mixed band rewards clear scope control, institutional evidence, and acknowledgment of disorder, but reflects overconfident identification and failure to answer representativeness, transmission, and plural-continuity objections.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-selective-hostile-source-survival","time":"38:10","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Untampered hostile writings may remain unrepresentative if Christian preservation favored pagan texts whose contents proved useful to later Christians.","score":83,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply distinguishes textual authenticity from representative survival, arguing that an untampered hostile document may still be nonrepresentative if Christian copyists preferentially preserved testimony useful to Christian claims. Principal limitation: The excerpt supplies neither manuscripts, comparative survival patterns, preservation history, nor evidence that useful hostile writings endured more often than unfavorable or irrelevant pagan material. Live burden: The skeptic must show that this selection mechanism is historically probable enough to weaken the independence of witnesses without converting every archival absence into evidence of Christian filtering. Locked score: The strong band recognizes conceptual sophistication, direct engagement with representativeness, and carefully qualified possibility, while withholding greater credit because the mechanism, scope, and documentary basis remain materially underdeveloped.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"section-early-orthodoxy-and-church-structure","title":"Early Orthodoxy, Diversity, and Ecclesial Structure","timebox":"25:49–49:23","score":{"pro":79,"con":82},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-syriac-roman-substantive-convergence","time":"42:36","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Roman and Syriac churches use different formulations yet share substantial Trinitarian, Christological, Marian, and incarnational commitments.","score":80,"critique":"Strongest feature: The response tests claimed regional divergence against doctrine, adducing Trinitarian theology, joint Christological declarations, and shared convictions about Christ and Mary while recognizing that linguistic formulations need not represent incompatible beliefs. Principal limitation: Modern or later agreements cannot establish continuous identity from Christianity’s early centuries, and the excerpt leaves chronology, institutional transmission, disagreements, representativeness among Syriac communions, and the historical relevance of retrospective convergence underspecified. Live burden: The affirmative must connect doctrinal compatibility to traceable historical continuity without minimizing conciliar, political, or institutional separation. Locked score: The strong/competent band reflects concrete comparative evidence, charitable attention to linguistic difference, and responsive engagement, tempered by compressed chronology, incomplete institutional analysis, and historical generalizations broader than the examples substantiate.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-preconciliar-christian-plurality","time":"25:49","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Numerous competing early Christian groups make preconciliar orthodoxy uncertain, with councils later establishing formal doctrinal boundaries.","score":77,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply directly challenges determinate continuity by foregrounding competing early communities and distinguishing the unsettled preconciliar landscape from later councils that formally articulated and enforced recognizable doctrinal boundaries. Principal limitation: Claiming nothing counted as orthodox before councils conflates formal recognition with substantive prior identity, while offering no representative groups, texts, prevalence evidence, institutional comparisons, or criteria for measuring the alleged plurality. Live burden: The skeptical route must show that early diversity defeats a developing proto-orthodox trajectory, rather than merely showing that trajectory faced competitors and lacked later precision. Locked score: The strong band reflects direct responsiveness and central relevance, constrained by terminological slippage, evidential generality, and an overbroad inference from conciliar definition to earlier indeterminacy.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-catholic-orthodox-theological-compatibility","time":"48:30","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Catholic and Orthodox theology can remain substantially compatible despite hostility, linguistic differences, and disputes over particular doctrinal formulations.","score":78,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply squarely addresses Eastern and Roman divergence through concrete disputed teachings, recognizes linguistic differences and hostility, and argues compatibility rather than complete agreement, institutional unity, or universal Orthodox acceptance. Principal limitation: References to many Orthodox believers lack scope, representative authorities, historical placement, and the qualifications attached to purgatory or the Immaculate Conception, leaving the breadth of convergence insufficiently documented. Live burden: The affirmative must establish how widespread and historically continuous these convergences are, while explaining why selected doctrinal compatibility outweighs ecclesiological and jurisdictional disagreements. Locked score: The strong band reflects relevant examples, substantial responsiveness, and complexity, tempered by unquantified representation, limited evidential support, and the inferential gap between theological compatibility and institutional continuity.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-winner-preservation-bias","time":"30:44","role":"Load-bearing constructive","words":"Surviving pre-Nicene evidence may disproportionately represent later winners because contrary materials could have disappeared or been destroyed.","score":86,"critique":"Strongest feature: The argument exposes representativeness problems in the surviving record, framing loss or destruction as a preservation hypothesis and supporting plausibility through recurring theological divergence after conciliar settlement. Principal limitation: The analogy neither quantifies missing materials nor identifies destruction or preservation mechanisms, and it cannot distinguish differential survival from deliberate winner-controlled selection or establish the magnitude and doctrinal direction of absent evidence. Live burden: The skeptic must connect plausible survivorship distortion to particular continuity claims while accounting for surviving heterodox texts and evidence that an identifiable tradition may have been institutionally dominant. Locked score: The very strong band reflects precise calibration, central relevance, charitable uncertainty, and a forceful representativeness challenge, despite limited documentary support for the proposed mechanism.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"section-apostolic-continuity-and-later-divisions","title":"Apostolic Continuity, Reform, and Later Christian Divisions","timebox":"41:37–57:13","score":{"pro":79,"con":81},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-apostolic-church-shared-core","time":"44:03","role":"Load-bearing constructive","words":"Catholic, Orthodox, and Syriac churches share central doctrine and conciliar inheritance despite incomplete uniformity and major institutional divisions.","score":87,"critique":"Strongest feature: By grounding commonality in shared Christology and conciliar inheritance, expressly conceding incomplete uniformity, and describing an apostolic family rather than an administratively identical institution, the reply answers the regional-divergence objection. Principal limitation: Seven shared councils does not straightforwardly characterize every Syriac communion, while papal authority, conciliar reception, and historical transmission into later churches remain underdeveloped. Live burden: The affirmative must establish that substantive doctrinal commonality and institutional ancestry together constitute durable apostolic continuity without illegitimately converting qualified family resemblance into singular institutional identity. Locked score: The very strong band recognizes responsiveness, qualification, charity, and concrete doctrinal support, while retaining reservations about conciliar variation and the bridge from shared inheritance to specifically Catholic continuity.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-jewish-sect-and-syrian-divergence","time":"41:37","role":"Load-bearing constructive","words":"Jewish Christian groups and divergent Syrian traditions complicate a single institutional and doctrinal path from earliest Christianity to Catholicism.","score":75,"critique":"Strongest feature: The argument expands the continuity inquiry beyond Roman evidence by invoking classification as a Jewish sect and Syrian communities, thereby pressing the affirmative to explain regional variation before claiming a single institutional trajectory. Principal limitation: These examples lack chronology, representativeness, doctrinal characterization, institutional relationships, and reason why divergence signifies independent origin or discontinuity rather than development, schism, or plural expression within an apostolic inheritance. Live burden: The skeptic must establish that the communities generate incompatible lineage claims or substantive discontinuity, rather than merely confirming linguistically unsurprising diversity. Locked score: The strong/competent band recognizes direct relevance and comparative pressure on linear continuity, while preserving reservations about historical substantiation and criteria separating disqualifying divergence from plural development.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-reform-versus-doctrinal-departure","time":"46:44","role":"Supporting reply","words":"Earlier reformers retained characteristic Catholic teachings, whereas Reformation traditions progressively discarded them and became substantially different systems.","score":67,"critique":"Strongest feature: By identifying Marian teachings, purgatory, and prayer for the dead while acknowledging Luther’s reforming intention, the move supplies concrete doctrinal markers and avoids reducing Protestant departure to a motive-based narrative. Principal limitation: It provides no principled threshold for when doctrinal subtraction becomes a distinct religious system, privileges Catholic markers, and leaves the Jewish-Christian and Syrian regional-divergence challenge unanswered. Live burden: The affirmative must defend its individuation criterion, compare rival accounts of Christianity’s doctrinal core, and connect later reform boundaries to ancient institutional continuity. Locked score: The mixed band recognizes relevant examples and a recoverable historical contrast, but reflects low responsiveness, unstable terminology, limited charity toward alternatives, and conclusions extending beyond the evidence.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-protestant-branches-and-individuation","time":"54:48","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Shared scripture and history support classifying Protestant traditions as Christian branches, while doctrinal magnitude alone cannot settle religious individuation.","score":86,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply directly challenges the separate-religion taxonomy by identifying shared scripture and history, acknowledging substantial Catholic-Protestant differences, and exposing the need for principled individuation criteria rather than allowing perceived doctrinal magnitude to determine classification intuitively. Principal limitation: The broader world-religion comparison remains schematic and does not specify which historical, textual, doctrinal, institutional, or self-identification criteria should control when those considerations conflict, so branch classification is plausible without being uniquely demonstrated. Live burden: The skeptic must articulate a consistent taxonomy and explain how shared inheritance bears on the narrower historical question of Catholic continuity rather than merely locating Protestantism inside Christianity generally. Locked score: The very strong band reflects exceptional multi-target responsiveness, stable qualification, representational charity, and coherent positive commonalities, notwithstanding limited theoretical development of the proposed individuation standard.","tags":[]}}]}],"overall":{"pro":{"score":76,"strengths":["Established an early Pauline foundation while carefully limiting the claim to evidence of Paul's bodily-resurrection belief.","Directly engaged the visionary reading through First Corinthians 15 and a transformed-body interpretation.","Used concrete doctrinal and conciliar commonalities to support convergence among ancient apostolic churches.","Regularly acknowledged incomplete uniformity, linguistic difference, and contested reform intentions."],"blunders":[{"text":"The Gospel and patristic convergence reply did not answer the decisive unknown-source, copying, selection, and independence objections.","links":[]},{"text":"The reform-versus-departure argument lacked a principled criterion for when doctrinal change creates a distinct religious system.","links":[]},{"text":"Several continuity claims moved from selected doctrinal compatibility to broad historical or institutional conclusions without a fully traced lineage.","links":[]}]},"con":{"score":83,"strengths":["Precisely distinguished early resurrection belief from evidence for a bodily encounter.","Separated Paul's personal experience from secondhand witness reports and carefully calibrated interpretive influence as a possibility.","Developed distinct objections from compositional delay, unknown sources, copying uncertainty, and selective preservation.","Pressed early Christian plurality and regional divergence against a simple linear continuity narrative.","Answered the Protestant-departure taxonomy with shared inheritance and an explicit individuation challenge."],"blunders":[{"text":"Several preservation arguments remained plausible hypotheses without named documentary examples or comparative survival evidence.","links":[]},{"text":"The preconciliar plurality argument risked conflating later formal definition with the prior nonexistence of an identifiable doctrinal trajectory.","links":[]},{"text":"The transmission objections were sometimes directed at Pauline claims they did not specifically contact.","links":[]}]}},"logicalExtension":{"pro":{"finalArgument":{"thesis":"The strongest affirmative case is a modest cumulative inference: early embodied-resurrection belief and independently traceable institutional and doctrinal continuities make bodily resurrection and Catholic continuity historically credible, although neither conclusion follows from any single source.","premises":["The chronology of undisputed Pauline correspondence makes resurrection belief early, while the distinction between Paul's own experience and inherited witness reports identifies multiple testimonial layers even if it does not guarantee complete independence.","A bodily reading should rest on contextual criteria such as continuity between burial and resurrection, transformation rather than replacement, and corporate resurrection language, not on the unsupported assertion that no alternative reading exists.","Each Pauline, Gospel, patristic, and hostile-witness claim should be assigned a date, information pathway, attested content, and dependence status before its contribution is included in the cumulative case.","Continuity should be tested through several invariants, including episcopal structure, sacramental practice, conciliar participation, and central Christological commitments, while allowing regional development and genuine institutional division."],"conclusion":"If the embodied reading, source-independence matrix, and continuity invariants survive detailed historical testing, the evidence can make bodily resurrection belief and Catholic continuity credible without claiming that the surviving record compels either conclusion."},"newArguments":[{"title":"Dependence-sensitive cumulative confirmation","text":"A cumulative case need not treat every source as independent or equally reliable. It can discount shared traditions, copying uncertainty, and later preservation while asking whether the residual evidence is still more expected if embodied-resurrection belief arose early than if it emerged only through late legendary development. This framework turns Oppy's objections into explicit evidential discounts instead of ignoring them, and it prevents Albrecht from gaining artificial corroboration merely by counting texts."},{"title":"Branching rather than linear continuity","text":"Institutional continuity can be modeled as a branching genealogy rather than a single uninterrupted administrative line. Under that model, Catholic, Orthodox, and Syriac divisions do not erase shared ancestry, but Catholic specificity still requires additional evidence about which structures and practices persist through the Roman branch. This approach incorporates early plurality and later schism without treating every divergence as either total discontinuity or irrelevant variation."}]},"con":{"finalArgument":{"thesis":"The strongest skeptical case is comparative rather than merely negative: the surviving evidence remains substantially expected under visionary experience, inherited interpretation, ordinary textual dependence, selective preservation, and branching institutional development, so it does not securely identify bodily encounters or specifically Catholic continuity.","premises":["Early evidence that Paul believed in resurrection does not by itself establish whether his experience was sensory, visionary, internally generated, or interpreted through traditions he had already received.","Transmission uncertainty should be applied locally: confidence decreases where a claim depends on unidentified sources, late composition, opaque selection, or missing comparators, without assuming that every surviving passage is corrupted.","Historical conclusions drawn from surviving orthodox and hostile materials should be tested under alternative preservation assumptions, including differential copying, accidental loss, institutional usefulness, and the known survival of some dissenting texts.","Conciliar definition may formalize an earlier trajectory, but that possibility does not establish that the trajectory was uniquely authoritative, geographically representative, or institutionally identical with later Catholicism."],"conclusion":"Unless the affirmative can discriminate bodily encounter from visionary interpretation, demonstrate source-level independence, and trace specifically Catholic institutional continuity through early plurality, historical confidence should remain limited rather than converted into either categorical denial or secure identification."},"newArguments":[{"title":"Explicit historical model selection","text":"The parties can compare rival historical models by listing what each predicts: bodily encounters, sincere visions interpreted through inherited expectation, gradual legendary elaboration, or mixed development. Evidence that every model predicts, such as early sincere belief, has limited discriminatory force. Greater weight should go to evidence that differs across models, including the form of appearance reports, independence of testimonial chains, and whether physical details appear early or accumulate later."},{"title":"Counterfactual test for Catholic specificity","text":"A useful continuity test asks whether the cited evidence would look substantially different if several ancient churches shared apostolic ancestry but later developed competing authority structures. Shared Christology, sacraments, and councils may be expected on that branching hypothesis as well as on specifically Catholic continuity. The affirmative therefore needs Roman-specific, temporally linked evidence that is not equally explained by common inheritance followed by institutional divergence."}]}}};
